@page "/learn/virtualize-anchor-mode"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Lists that follow the newest item · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Lists that follow the newest item</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">New messages arrive at the bottom. Does your list show them, or leave them out of sight?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>Virtualize</code> has an <code>AnchorMode</code> parameter. With
        <code>VirtualizeAnchorMode.End</code>, the list follows new items while you're at the bottom, and stops when
        you scroll up. In .NET 10, <code>Virtualize</code> could show a long list but never followed its end.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a support inbox. It loads 300 messages, #10900 to #11199, some of them long. Then a new one arrives
        every 2.5 seconds. The list is a plain <code>Virtualize</code> (it only renders the rows in view) in a 300px
        box. This is the .NET 10 code:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 list</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div class="feed" style="height: 300px; overflow-y: auto"&gt;
    <span class="err">&lt;Virtualize Items="messages" Context="m"&gt;</span>
        ...
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

<span class="dim">// every 2.5 seconds</span>
messages.Add(InboxMessage.Make(messages.Count));
StateHasChanged();</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The site runs on .NET 11, but a <code>Virtualize</code> without <code>AnchorMode</code> behaves just as it did
        in .NET 10. So Fig. 1 shows the .NET 10 behaviour.
    </p>
    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The inbox, with a plain Virtualize" Url="demo/anchor-mode/inbox/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: plain Virtualize" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            It opens on the newest message. Just watch for a few seconds: new messages arrive below the box, and the
            list stays where it is. Scroll down to find them.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Virtualize keeps your scroll position</h2>
    <p>When a message is added, the list grows below you, and the scroll position stays put:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Adding a message to a list you've scrolled to the bottom</span>
        <pre class="snip">┌ the box, 300px ───────────┐
│ #11197                    │
│ #11198                    │
│ #11199                    │  <span class="ok">at the bottom</span>
└───────────────────────────┘
  #11200  <span class="dim">messages.Add(...)</span>        <span class="err">below the fold</span>
  #11201  <span class="dim">messages.Add(...)</span>        <span class="err">below the fold</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        That's right for someone reading older messages, but not for someone waiting for the next one.
        <code>Virtualize</code> can't tell them apart, so it never scrolls.
    </p>
    <p>
        Upgrading to .NET 11 doesn't change this. An existing list won't follow its end until you opt in.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Anchor the list to its end</h2>
    <p>Add one parameter to the same <code>Virtualize</code>. You add items exactly as before.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div class="feed" style="height: 300px; overflow-y: auto"&gt;
    &lt;Virtualize Items="messages" Context="m"
                <span class="hl">AnchorMode="VirtualizeAnchorMode.End"</span>&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        With <code>End</code>, the list follows new items only while you're at the bottom (within 2px). Scroll away and
        it stops, so it never pulls a reader back down. Blazor does the scrolling itself, so you write no JavaScript,
        and there's no extra trip between browser and server for each message.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same inbox, anchored to the end" Url="demo/anchor-mode/inbox"
                Label=".NET 11: AnchorMode.End">
        <Caption>
            It opens on the newest message too. Just watch: each new message scrolls into view as it arrives. Scroll up
            a little and it stops following, so what you're reading stays put. Scroll back down and it follows again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>The two lists differ by one parameter. Here's the anchored list, then its page, then the .NET 10 list:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AnchorMode/MessageFeed.razor" Feature="virtualize-anchor-mode">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's list. Only the <code>AnchorMode</code> line is new. The other highlighted lines, marked
            <i>Not new</i> in their comments, are what you keep: the box with a height, and the plain <code>Add</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AnchorMode/SupportInbox.razor" Feature="virtualize-anchor-mode">
        <Caption>
            The page is static. Only the list is interactive, because it has to scroll as each message arrives. The highlighted render
            mode line isn't new either.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AnchorMode/MessageFeedNet10.razor" Feature="virtualize-anchor-mode">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's list: the same messages and the same timer, without <code>AnchorMode</code>. Its small script
            runs once, to open the list at the newest message. It isn't the follow-the-end JavaScript below, and
            nothing keeps the list at the bottom.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Your own JavaScript, or no virtualizing</h2>
    <p>
        Apps tracked the bottom themselves: ask JavaScript whether the reader is at the bottom, add the message, then
        scroll down if they were.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to follow the end in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">var atBottom = await JS.InvokeAsync&lt;bool&gt;(
    <span class="err">"chat.isAtBottom"</span>, scroller);
messages.Add(message);
StateHasChanged();
if (atBottom) await JS.InvokeVoidAsync(
    <span class="err">"chat.scrollToBottom"</span>, scroller);</pre>
                <span class="why">in our test it followed three messages. Then a tall one arrived, and the scroll stopped 72px above the bottom. From then on <code>isAtBottom</code> said no, so it never followed again</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a call to JavaScript and back for every message, and it breaks when messages differ in height</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@foreach (var message in messages)
{
    &lt;ChatMessage Message="message" /&gt;
}</pre>
                <span class="why">every message stays on the page, with the same JavaScript to scroll down</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a busy feed gets slower as the day goes on</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        <b>If your app has the first one, delete it when you upgrade.</b> When we ran the same JavaScript on .NET 11, without
        <code>AnchorMode</code>, it stopped following at the very first new message, sooner than on .NET 10. Set
        <code>AnchorMode</code> instead.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One parameter does the scrolling</h2>
    <p>
        Fig. 2 uses <code>End</code>, for a list that grows at the bottom. <code>VirtualizeAnchorMode</code> has two
        other values. <code>Start</code> is the default. It's for lists with the newest item at the top: if you're at
        the top when items are added there, they stay in view. It does nothing for items added at the end. That's why a list
        without <code>AnchorMode</code>, like Fig. 1, still doesn't follow. <code>None</code> turns this off, so the list never scrolls by itself. You can also
        combine them: <code>Start | End</code> follows new items at whichever end you're at.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>On .NET 10, <code>AnchorMode</code> builds, then the page returns a 500.</b> Razor lets the unknown attribute through without a warning. At runtime, <code>Virtualize</code> "does not have a property matching the name 'AnchorMode'".</li>
        <li><b>Write <code>VirtualizeAnchorMode.End</code>, not <code>End</code>.</b> A bare <code>AnchorMode="End"</code> doesn't compile.</li>
        <li><b>Loading rows with <code>ItemsProvider</code>? Set <code>ItemComparer</code> too.</b> Make it compare rows by id. Each query returns new objects, even for rows you already have. Without the comparer, the list can't tell they're the same rows, treats them as new rows added above, and stops following. Analyzer BL0011 warns you about this.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
